{
  "an4.30:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 4.30 ",
  "an4.30:0.2": "3. At Uruvelā ",
  "an4.30:0.3": "Wanderers ",
  "an4.30:1.1": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha, on the Vulture’s Peak Mountain. ",
  "an4.30:1.2": "Now at that time several very well-known wanderers were residing in their monastery on the bank of the Serpentine River. They included Annabhāra, Varadhara, Sakuludāyī, and other very well-known wanderers. ",
  "an4.30:1.3": "Then in the late afternoon, the Buddha came out of retreat and went to the wanderer’s monastery on the bank of the Serpentine River. He sat down on the seat spread out and said to the wanderers: ",
  "an4.30:2.1": "“Wanderers, these four footprints of the Dhamma are primordial, long-standing, customary, and traditional. They are uncorrupted, as they have been since the beginning. They’re not being corrupted now nor will they be. Sensible ascetics and brahmins don’t look down on them. ",
  "an4.30:2.2": "What four? ",
  "an4.30:2.3": "Contentment … ",
  "an4.30:2.4": "Good will … Right mindfulness … Right immersion … ",
  "an4.30:2.5": "These four footprints of the Dhamma are primordial, long-standing, customary, and traditional. They are uncorrupted, as they have been since the beginning. They’re not being corrupted now nor will they be. Sensible ascetics and brahmins don’t look down on them. ",
  "an4.30:3.1": "Wanderers, if someone should say: ",
  "an4.30:3.2": "‘I’ll deny this Dhamma footprint of contentment, and describe a true 